+# Guest builds
+
+Run the generic builder from this checkout's root:
+
+```sh
+bash scripts/guest.sh umpire-stock-guest build/stock.wasm
+bash scripts/guest.sh umpire-stock-guest build/stock.wasm --check
+bash scripts/guest-freshness.sh umpire-stock-guest build/stock.wasm
+bash scripts/tests/build-guests.sh
+```
+
+The stock guest selects its Kettle Model at build time. A consumer guest chooses
+its own Model and realization; the runtime library itself exports no application
+functions. Generic stock, stateful, caught-panic, overflow and Kettle guests are
+built locally for controls and are not committed or stored in Git LFS. Consumer
+publication and embedded assets belong to the consumer.
+
+The builder requires installed local mise pins, Rust sources and wasm32-wasip1.
+It uses `--locked` and `profile.guest`: release inheritance, stripped symbols,
+no debug information, fat LTO, one codegen unit and checked overflow. The ordinary
+release checker profile keeps profiling symbols. Missing tools and compiler
+failures fail for every `CI` value. Bash only bootstraps and delegates;
+[the native driver contract](dev/guest-driver/README.md) owns metadata, identity
+and freshness semantics.
+
+## Caller context and output
+
+`scripts/xtask.sh` builds the native CLI and dedicated guest driver before guest
+flags and wrappers, into `<target>/xtask-bootstrap`. The executable `guest-rustc`
+is an atomic copy of the driver, dispatched by its executable name. It preserves
+raw compiler arguments, prior wrappers, jobserver descriptors and loader variables
+without recursive Cargo launches. The bootstrap disables mise automatic installs.
+
+Relative outputs and explicit target overrides resolve against the original
+caller workspace. The default target is this framework's `target/` for local
+builds. Each consumer retains its own workspace/lockfile; framework and caller
+source roots are admitted separately. Metadata and builds receive the caller's
+explicit manifest. See [consumer invocation](docs/consumer-interface.md).
+
+Cargo artifact JSON must identify exactly one cdylib wasm file, including custom
+library names. Missing, malformed, multiple or unreadable outputs fail before
+publication. Builds publish by sibling rename; checks never update the destination.
+Status 0 means built/fresh, 1 means tool/input/output failure, and 2 means missing
+or stale output. A stale diagnostic includes sizes and the first differing byte
+offset, including EOF. Compiler failures preserve their exit status.
+
+## Portable identity
+
+The native driver remaps caller, separately admitted framework, sysroot, registry
+and target paths through one canonical longest-root mapping. Symbol identity
+covers compiler input, normalized package/source configuration and dependency
+identities. No bytes are rewritten after linking.
+
+Lock schema 2 hashes each crate's sorted non-development dependency closure,
+including build and platform-conditional edges, rather than the entire lockfile.
+Portable coordinates omit checkout-bearing Cargo IDs. Ambiguous package
+coordinates, missing closures and missing registry lock entries refuse. Whole
+workspace feature resolution can intentionally over-hash shared optional edges;
+unrelated/dev-only additions preserve bytes only when resolved guest closures
+remain unchanged. Build-std exemptions require pinned sysroot inputs.
+
+Cargo keeps its own artifact names and fingerprints. Atomic `.umpire-id` sidecars
+publish before the compiler starts, support pipelining, serialize per output and
+are removed after compiler failure. Persistent lock files remain in place.
+Portable proc-macro identities retain host-variant collision controls. Guest cache
+namespaces include dedicated driver bytes, full caller lockfile and compiler
+input; unrelated general CLI bytes are outside that identity. Native schema 2
+replaces old Python records and invalidates old sidecars; historical JSON/TOML
+quirks and old wasm bytes are not compatibility contracts.
+
+## Caught panic and host admission
+
+The shipped wasm standard library aborts panics. The builder rebuilds `std` and
+`panic_unwind` from pinned sources using command-scoped `RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP=1`,
+`-Zbuild-std=std,panic_unwind`, `-Cpanic=unwind`,
+`-Ctarget-feature=+exception-handling` and
+`-Cllvm-args=-wasm-use-legacy-eh=false`. This uses unstable interfaces on a pinned
+stable compiler. A toolchain upgrade requires new panic and reproduction controls.
+
+The host uses wazero's interpreter with final exception handling enabled. It
+admits only exact additional WASI `fd_close` and `fd_seek` signatures; both return
+BADF without accessing descriptors or output memory. It installs no general WASI
+module. Memory limits, cancellation, serialization, import/export signatures and
+invalidation after an uncaught trap remain enforced. Checker-caught panics refute
+the step while leaving the instance usable. Native/wasm parity controls compare
+complete responses, repeated calls, Reset and the existing report/row goldens.
+Current guests declare WHOLE_PREFIX_V1 during Reset; missing or unknown contracts
+refuse before Driver creation. The model hash binds the actual loaded bytes.
+
+## Current verification commands
+
+```sh
+mise exec -- cargo test -p xtask -p umpire-guest-driver --locked
+CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/absolute/task-owned-target bash scripts/tests/build-guests.sh
+CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/absolute/task-owned-target UMPIRE_APALACHE=require make validate
+```
+
+`make validate` runs the TLC-required full gate, isolated standalone proof and
+edited-checker timing. Apalache remains a separate required-mode selection.
+The 60 s gate and 10 s inner-loop budgets are soft targets; report overruns.
+Two clean source clones at distinct absolute paths with independent initially
+empty targets are needed to prove path independence for a particular input.
+Compare actual wasm bytes and record source, toolchain, platform, sizes and hashes.
+Same-OS reproduction alone does not establish cross-OS identity.
+
+## Historical reproduction evidence
+
+The following evidence belongs to the original source history and is not current
+standalone certification. Original `model/GUEST_BUILDS.md` at extraction source
+`c88fdc5506` records exact runners, receipts, superseding inputs and hashes;
+`.flow/` evidence paths below identify that source repository only.
+
+| Source / task | Recorded result |
+| --- | --- |
+| `8d429e97409b1537ecb347c359129bffafa63f78`, fn-232, 2026-10-21 | Linux/aarch64 and macOS/arm64 each passed two-clone path comparisons, but all cross-OS comparisons failed (first offsets stock 443, system guest 635). |
+| `a96a4c6550b468c5d7c3011a7961d4e118dacd93`, fn-239, 2026-10-22 | Four clean clones with empty targets passed all twelve byte comparisons and both host suites, using Rust 1.99.1 / LLVM 23.1.1, Go 1.27.0 and protoc 29.5. Stock: 192489 bytes, SHA-256 `bae203f82ecea5183d592232c62b746f6ccabda9944df53e46dd442482d1bc63`. Proof: `.flow/artifacts/fn-239-renew-the-linuxmacos-guest-byte/certification-a96a4c6550/proof.md`. |
+| `523d131d2cf992b04a43b4d595ea0f1a434f44b6`, fn-247 | Native identity schema 2: two clean Linux/aarch64 clones with independent targets agreed on stock and system guests. Cold/cached freshness and stale status 2 were exercised. This did not certify native macOS/cross-OS behavior or a complete gate. Proof: `.flow/artifacts/fn-247-one-language-for-the-framework-a-rust/task5/joined/proof-report.md`. |
+
+Later consumer-closure changes regenerated the system-owned embedded guest; its
+hashes are retained by that consumer, not a shipped generic artifact. Earlier
+proofs retain their own pins and cannot certify a changed driver, dependency
+closure or compiler. Original evidence and unavailable older ancestry are
+provenance, never prerequisites for building this checkout.
